<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=de-motu-cordis">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">De Motu Cordis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1628">1628</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Exercitatio Anatomica de Motu Cordis et Sanguinis in Animalibus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">De Motu Cordis is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-harvey">William Harvey</a>'s treatise on the motion of the heart and blood, first published in 1628, which established that the blood circulates through the body. Treating the heart as a pump and measuring the blood it sends out, Harvey concluded that the same blood keeps returning in a closed circuit, out through the arteries and back by the veins. He had presented the case in lectures by 1616; in 1660 Marcello Malpighi saw blood moving in the capillaries, supplying the missing link in the proof.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/de-motu-cordis.jpg" alt="De Motu Cordis" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Harvey_(1578-1657)_Venenbild.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title means An Anatomical Exercise on the Motion of the Heart and Blood in Living Beings. William Harvey, a royal physician, had studied arts and medicine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in the early 1600s, where he furthered his training in anatomy under Hieronymus Fabricius ab Acquapendente, and received his MD degree in April 1602.</p><p>Others had come part of the way before him. In the thirteenth century Ibn Al-Nafis proposed the pulmonary circulation; in 1553 Michael Servetus said that blood flows from the heart to the lungs and mixes there with air; between 1570 and 1590 Cesalpino suggested, against the Galenists, that the blood moved in something more like a circulation than an oscillation; and in 1603 Fabricius described the valves of the veins, which hinder blood from flowing away from the heart.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Harvey investigated the effect of ligatures on blood flow and argued for a double circulation: blood returned to the heart is sent through the lungs and back to the heart before it passes into the main circulation. His quantitative argument rested on the output of each beat. He observed that two ounces of blood leave the heart with each beat, so that at 72 beats a minute it throws 540 pounds of blood into the system every hour, an amount that could only be the same blood returning. The one-way valves of the heart and veins showed the direction of the flow. As Harvey put it, &quot;There must be a motion, as it were, in a circle.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book is counted a landmark in the history of physiology, both for the discovery of the complete circulation and for its experimental, quantitative and mechanistic method, which treated the heart not as a mystical seat of the spirit and faculties but as a pump. It had an immediate and far-reaching influence on Harvey's contemporaries: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a> said that Harvey was the only modern author whose doctrines were taught in his lifetime. The one stage Harvey could not see, where arteries and veins divide into capillaries, was observed in a frog's lung three years after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Exercitatio Anatomica de Motu Cordis et Sanguinis in Animalibus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1628</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/william-harvey">William Harvey</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Ibn al-Nafis had described the pulmonary circulation in the 13th century and Michael Servetus in 1553; the complete circulation is Harvey’s.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/de-humani-corporis-fabrica">Modern anatomy<span>1543</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/blood-transfusion">Blood transfusion<span>1665</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=de-motu-cordis">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
